"The first five verses of this surah are the first verses of the Qur'an to be revealed. Surah al-Fatihah, on the other hand, is the first to be revealed as a complete surah. The first revelation came to our Prophet in the cave of Hira; Gabriel said to him 'Read,' and he replied 'I cannot read.' This repetition occurred three times, and finally the verse 'Read in the name of your Lord!' was revealed. That these five verses were the first to be revealed is established by authentic narrations. The remainder of the surah was revealed later, specifically upon the transgression of Abu Jahl.\n\nThe first five verses are as follows: 'Read in the name of your Lord who created! He created man from an alaq (embryo). Read! And your Lord is the Most Generous. Who taught by the pen. Taught man what he did not know.' In these verses, Allah's creation, teaching, and generosity are emphasized. Although our Prophet was unlettered (ummi—unable to read and write), Allah granted him the ability to read and revealed the Qur'an to him. The teaching of writing with the pen is also among Allah's great blessings.\n\nFrom the sixth verse onward, the warning begins: 'No! Indeed, man transgresses, because he sees himself self-sufficient (free of need). Indeed, to your Lord is the return.' Here, it is reminded that man transgresses by considering himself self-sufficient, but in the end he will return to Allah. This address is directed first to our Prophet, and then to all people.\n\nAfterward, Abu Jahl's preventing our Prophet from performing prayer is recounted: 'Have you seen the one who prevents a servant when he prays? If he is upon guidance, or commands righteousness? What if he denies the truth and turns away? Does that man not know that Allah sees him?' The verses condemn this transgression of Abu Jahl and issue a warning to him. Scholars of tafsir mention Abu Jahl's attempt to prevent the Prophet (peace be upon him) from prayer as the occasion of revelation for these verses.\n\nAllah the Almighty directs a severe threat toward Abu Jahl: 'No, no! If he does not desist, We will surely drag him by the forelock—that lying, sinning forelock. Then let him call his associates; We will call the angels of torment (zabaniyah).' The zabaniyah are the angels of punishment who are the keepers of Hell. Here, the power of Allah is emphasized in contrast to the power of the prominent figures of the age of ignorance (Jahiliyyah).\n\nThe final verse of the surah is a comfort and a command to our Prophet: 'No, never obey him! Prostrate and draw near.' That is, do not comply with transgressors like Abu Jahl, continue your servitude to your Lord, and draw near to Him through prostration. Prostration is the moment when the servant is closest to Allah. This verse also requires a prostration of recitation (tilawah); our Prophet also prostrated when he recited this surah."